[
    {
        "content": "<p>It is often written that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f: x → y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span></span></span></span>. Is this the same as <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f ∈ \\mathcal C [x, y]</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0583em;\">C</span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span></span></span>? If so, can I choose one and use it exclusively? For example, can I say either <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>:</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">C</mi><mo>→</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"script\">C</mi><mtext>op</mtext></msup><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y: \\mathcal C → (\\mathcal C^{\\text {op}} → \\mathcal S)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0583em;\">C</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0583em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">op</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.075em;\">S</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> or <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>:</mo><mtext>Cat</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">C</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext>Cat</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"script\">C</mi><mtext>op</mtext></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y: \\text{Cat}[\\mathcal C, \\text{Cat}[\\mathcal C^{\\text {op}}, \\mathcal S]]</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Cat</span></span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0583em;\">C</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Cat</span></span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0583em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">op</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.075em;\">S</span><span class=\"mclose\">]]</span></span></span></span> for the embedding of Yoneda?</p>",
        "id": 623817948,
        "sender_full_name": "Ignat Insarov",
        "timestamp": 1789273247
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Perhaps it would even be enough to write <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>:</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">C</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"script\">C</mi><mtext>op</mtext></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y: [\\mathcal C, [\\mathcal C^{\\text {op}}, \\mathcal S]]</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0583em;\">C</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0583em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">op</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.075em;\">S</span><span class=\"mclose\">]]</span></span></span></span>, without naming the category the arrows are to be taken from? After all, we always know what category our objects belong to, so there is no ambiguity.</p>",
        "id": 623818203,
        "sender_full_name": "Ignat Insarov",
        "timestamp": 1789273402
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I have not seen a situation where confusion arises from mixing the two notations, but I would say there are cases where one may feel a tiny bit weird using the second. The feeling I have is that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f : x \\to y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span></span></span></span> is a meta-linguistic expression that doesn't require you to commit to a flavour or foundation, and can mean, for example, that \"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span></span></span></span>\", whatever it is, is a proof that \"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x \\le y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7719em;vertical-align:-0.136em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≤</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span></span></span></span>\". At the same time, one could say that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f \\in \\mathcal C[x,y]</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0583em;\">C</span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span></span></span> because in a poset <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">C</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>≤</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\mathcal C,\\le)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0583em;\">C</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≤</span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> there is at most one element in each hom- <em>set</em>. The difference is almost imperceptible but I feel something has changed.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>After all, we always know what category our objects belong to, so there is no ambiguity. </p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Sometimes you don't: finite sets, but only isomorphisms/injections/surjections; finite sets, with partial functions; finite sets, and all functions; finite sets, with all relations, not just the functional ones; these are quite different categories. The difference is so noticeable that in the early days Ehresmann pushed to call a category with the name of its <em>arrows</em> rather than with the name of it objects. So, one should say <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">F</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">{\\bf pFun}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8805em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbf\">pFun</span></span></span></span></span></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">{\\bf Inj}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbf\">Inj</span></span></span></span></span></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">{\\bf Rel}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbf\">Rel</span></span></span></span></span></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">{\\bf Cont}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbf\">Cont</span></span></span></span></span></span> (topological spaces and continuous maps)...</p>",
        "id": 623842377,
        "sender_full_name": "fosco",
        "timestamp": 1789288635
    },
    {
        "content": "<blockquote>\n<p>in the early days Ehresmann pushed to call a category with the name of its <em>arrows</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Good to know, do you have a reference for this?</p>",
        "id": 623871350,
        "sender_full_name": "Ignat Insarov",
        "timestamp": 1789306243
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Mac Lane acknowledges it in the historical section of chapter 1 of CWM:<br>\n<img alt=\"image.png\" class=\"inline-image\" data-original-content-type=\"image/png\" data-original-dimensions=\"1359x367\" data-original-src=\"/user_uploads/21317/qPgIo3A99Ms1RmmEd89EPRUo/image.png\" src=\"/user_uploads/thumbnail/21317/qPgIo3A99Ms1RmmEd89EPRUo/image.png/840x560.webp\"></p>",
        "id": 623872595,
        "sender_full_name": "fosco",
        "timestamp": 1789307134
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><img alt=\"image.png\" class=\"inline-image\" data-original-content-type=\"image/png\" data-original-dimensions=\"1225x118\" data-original-src=\"/user_uploads/21317/okU3t_H5mW39vsYTNI08vScF/image.png\" src=\"/user_uploads/thumbnail/21317/okU3t_H5mW39vsYTNI08vScF/image.png/840x560.webp\"></p>",
        "id": 623872666,
        "sender_full_name": "fosco",
        "timestamp": 1789307187
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I would say \"Catégories et Structures\" is not easy to find; I saw only one copy, not in good condition, in the university library back in 2010.</p>",
        "id": 623872748,
        "sender_full_name": "fosco",
        "timestamp": 1789307248
    },
    {
        "content": "<blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>After all, we always know what category our objects belong to, so there is no ambiguity.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Sometimes you don't: finite sets, but only isomorphisms/injections/surjections; finite sets, with partial functions; finite sets, and all functions; finite sets, with all relations, not just the functional ones; these are quite different categories.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I was thinking that we should think of objects as formal symbols marked with the category they belong to. Otherwise, we should get into all kinds of trouble. For example, we want to have a functor <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>–</mtext><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext>–</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>:</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"script\">C</mi><mtext>op</mtext></msup><mo>×</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">C</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal C [–, –]: \\mathcal C^{\\text {op}} × \\mathcal C → \\mathcal S</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0583em;\">C</span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord\">–</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">–</span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0583em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">op</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0583em;\">C</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.075em;\">S</span></span></span></span>, and for that to happen we must have a function on objects that sends them to appropriate arrow sets. If objects are not labelled with the category they belong to, we cannot define that function.</p>",
        "id": 623872817,
        "sender_full_name": "Ignat Insarov",
        "timestamp": 1789307307
    },
    {
        "content": "<blockquote>\n<p>I would say \"Catégories et Structures\" is not easy to find; I saw only one copy, not in good condition, in the university library back in 2010.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>How about <em>Catégories structurées</em> from back in 1963? <a href=\"https://www.numdam.org/item/10.24033/asens.1125.pdf\">https://www.numdam.org/item/10.24033/asens.1125.pdf</a> It says, for instance:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>une catégorie d'homomorphismes à produits finis, au-dessus d'une catégorie <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"fraktur\">M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathfrak M</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6914em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathfrak\">M</span></span></span></span> d'applications</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is <em>«a category of homomorphisms with finite products on top of a category <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"fraktur\">M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathfrak M</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6914em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathfrak\">M</span></span></span></span> of functions»</em>.</p>",
        "id": 623874609,
        "sender_full_name": "Ignat Insarov",
        "timestamp": 1789308679
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Anna's Archive, which of course is illegal, has <em><a href=\"https://annas-archive.gl/md5/9dbc8ccd1c22b0bdf32efc7b91f7e902\">Catégories et structures: extraits</a></em>.</p>",
